# QUAKEALERT DEVELOPMENT RULES

## Project Identity & Architecture
- Domain & Package: `id.web.quakealert` (quakealert.web.id).
- Framework: Native Android with Jetpack Compose & Kotlin.
- Architecture: MVVM pattern with unidirectional data flow (UDF) using Kotlin Coroutines & StateFlow.
- SDK: Minimum SDK API 28 (or API 26 compatibility), targeting latest stable SDK 37.
- Build Tooling: Gradle Kotlin DSL (`build.gradle.kts`) with Version Catalogs (`libs.versions.toml`).

## UI & Design Guidelines
- Design Paradigm: Strict Custom Flat Minimalist Dark UI extracted from Figma tokens (deep pitch-black backgrounds, high-contrast typography, border accents, and rounded stadium/pill buttons). Avoid default standard Material 3 surfaces where custom dark styling is specified.
- Design System: All color tokens, typography styles, spacing values, and corner radiuses must be centralized in `ui/theme/` (`Color.kt`, `Type.kt`, `Theme.kt`, `Dimens.kt`). Do not hardcode hex colors or raw pixel dimensions inside Composable components.
- Navigation & Flow: Use `HorizontalPager` with custom indicator dots for multi-step onboarding flows. Ensure stateless Composable screens with state hoisting to their respective ViewModels.

## Figma-to-Code Workflow (via MCP)
- Always query the Figma MCP server to inspect the exact node tree, auto-layout properties, paddings (dp), text sizes (sp), and asset dimensions before writing Compose UI.
- Maintain pixel-perfect visual fidelity matching the Figma nodes.

## Quality Control & Verification
- Keep all Composables modular, decoupled, and testable with dedicated `@Preview` configurations.
- Always verify every change by executing `./gradlew assembleDebug` in the terminal to confirm zero build errors or unresolved references before completing tasks.

## Compose & Navigation Best Practices
- **Rule A (Layout & Insets)**: Never stack outer margins on a floating bottom bar in a way that pollutes `Scaffold.innerPadding`. A floating bar must only contribute its intended inset; use the `contentPadding` of the scrolling list (e.g. `LazyColumn`) as the single source of truth for the final content-to-bar spacing.
- **Rule B (Icon Integrity)**: Always lock vector drawable size with `Modifier.size()` and render with `ContentScale.Fit` (no stretch/fill manipulation) so vector proportions stay 1:1.
- **Rule C (Fading & Shaders)**: Use alpha masking (`CompositingStrategy.Offscreen` + `BlendMode.DstIn`) for edge-fade effects so they stay theme/background agnostic. Always hoist/`remember` `Brush` objects outside the draw phase to keep it allocation-free.
- **Rule D (Page Transitions)**: All destination changes must use `AnimatedContent` / `NavHost` transitions (standard Material 3 style) without blocking recomposition.

## Git & Version Control Automation

- **Atomic & Verified Commits**: Trigger a Git commit only after a logical unit of work is completed and successfully verified via `./gradlew assembleDebug` (never commit broken code).
- **Conventional Commits**: Use conventional commit messages format:
  - `feat: <description>` for new screens, components, or major capabilities.
  - `fix: <description>` for bug fixes, broken layouts, or logic corrections.
  - `refactor: <description>` for code optimization, hoisting, or restructuring without behavior changes.
  - `style: <description>` for Figma token alignments, spacing, and typography adjustments.
- **Push Policy**: Stage and commit locally after each milestone; only push to `origin main` when explicitly requested by the user or upon reaching a major flow milestone.